{{smaller block|themselves. Besides these essential parts there were the λογεῖον, ''proscenium'', or ''pulpitum'', the stage proper, and the ''postscenium'', or structure behind the stage, in which parts the Greek and Roman theaters differed considerably. Almost all surviving Greek theaters were profoundly modified in Roman times, but the original disposition can still be followed in several, as those of Epidaurus and Sicyon. Scenery, in the modern sense of the word, was little employed, but the stage machinery became elaborate with the advance of time. In the early days of the modern theater the buildings were only partially roofed, and the stage but scantily if at all provided with scenery. The interior of the theaters of the present day is usually constructed on a horseshoe or semicircular plan, with several tiers of galleries round the walls. The stage has a slight downward slope from the back, and is furnished with movable scenes, which give an air of reality to the spectacle which was unsought in the ancient theater. See ''box''<sup>2</sup>, ''curtain'', ''orchestra'', ''parquet'', ''pit'', ''postscenium'', ''proscenium'', ''scene'', ''stage'', ''stall''<sup>1</sup>, ''thymele''.}}

{{smaller block|As for their ''theaters'' in halfe circle, they came to be by the great magnificence of the Romain princes and people somptuously built with marble & square stone in forme all round, & were called Ampitheaters, wherof as yet appears one amōg the anciēt ruines of Rome.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 29.}}}}

{{smaller block|The world by some, & that not much amisse,
Vnto a ''Theater'' comparèd is,
Vpon which stage the goddes spectatours sitt,
And mortals act their partes as best doth fitt.
{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 126.}}}}

{{smaller block|As in a ''theater'' the eyes of men,
After a well grac'd Actor leaues the Stage,
Are idely bent on him that enters next.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II. (fol. 1623), v. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Sceaw-stow. A ''Theater'', a Shew-place, a beholding-place.
''Verstegan'', Restitution of Decayed Intelligence (ed. 1628),
{{right|[p. 231.}}}}

'''2.''' A room, hall, or other place, with a platform at one end, and ranks of seats rising stepwise as the tiers recede from the center, or

readings.c2s3:
otherwise so arranged that a body of spectators can have an unobstructed view of the platform. {{smaller|Places of this description are constructed for public lectures, academic exercises, anatomical demonstrations, surgical operations before a class, etc.: as, an operating ''theater''.}}

{{smaller block|Stately ''theatres'',
Bench'd crescent-wise. In each we sat, we heard
The grave Professor.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, ii.}}}}

'''3.''' A place rising by steps or gradations like the seats of a theater.

{{smaller block|Shade above shade, a woodie ''Theatre''
Of stateliest view.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L. (1st ed.), iv. 141.}}}}

{{smaller block|Helps the ambitious hill the heavens to scale,
Or scoops in circling ''theatres'' the vale.
{{right|''Pope'', Moral Essays, iv. 60.}}}}

'''4.''' A place of action or exhibition; a field of operations; the locality or scene where a series of events takes place or may be observed; scene; seat: as, the ''theater'' of war.

{{smaller block|Men must know that in this ''theatre'' of man's life it is reserved only for God and angels to be lookers on.
{{right|''Bacon'', Advancement of Learning, ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|This City was for a long time the ''Theatre'' of Contention between the Christians and Infidels.
{{right|''Maundrell'', Aleppo to Jerusalem, p. 54.}}}}

'''5.''' The drama; the mass of dramatic literature; also, theatrical representation; the stage: as, a history of the French ''theater''.

{{smaller block|But now our British ''theatre'' can boast
Drolls of all kinds, a vast, unthinking host!
{{right|''Addison'', Prol. to Steele's Tender Husband.}}}}

'''6.''' An amphitheater; hence, a circular reservoir or receptacle; a basin. [Rare.]

